---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import OptionsLab from "../../components/OptionsLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Options Pricing, Demystified"
  description="Build intuition for what an option is actually worth. Drag the strike, the time to expiry, and the volatility, and watch the Black–Scholes price and payoff diagram respond, so calls and puts finally click."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>What is an option actually worth?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      An option is the right, but not the obligation, to buy (a <em>call</em>) or
      sell (a <em>put</em>) a stock at a set price. Its value comes from three
      levers: how far the stock sits from the strike, how much time is left, and
      how wildly the stock moves. Drag them and watch the price, the payoff, and
      the break-even respond.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <OptionsLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Reading an option price</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Intrinsic + time value.</strong> Every option price is what it
          would be worth exercised today (intrinsic value) plus a premium for what
          might still happen before expiry (time value). Time value is highest at
          the money and decays to zero as expiration nears.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Time and volatility create the value.</strong> Because your loss
          is capped at the premium but your gain isn't, a wider range of possible
          prices is worth more. More time and more volatility both widen that
          range, which is why calm stocks have cheap options and volatile ones dear.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The strike sets the shape.</strong> A call pays off above the
          strike, a put below it. The break-even is the strike shifted by the
          premium you paid, the price the stock must reach before you're ahead.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Options are a genuine tool for hedging and expressing views, but they are
        unforgiving: most expire worthless, and leverage cuts both ways. Nothing
        here is a recommendation to trade them. Educational only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="options" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/options" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
</style>
